WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.500 --> 00:00:10.000
If we think together about the benefit I can get from a pattern like this: why is there a pattern called the stepper at all? If we look at examples like

2
00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:17.500
these and start thinking, we can come up with some benefits: I won't need to use the keyboard, I can just tap, increasing and decreasing until I reach the number I want.

3
00:00:17.500 --> 00:00:29.250
If I want to make the most of this, I need to understand the default of the input the user enters. Say I'm building a website or mobile app for people to book flights:

4
00:00:29.250 --> 00:00:41.000
I'll look at how many adults users enter when they book, how many people they book tickets for: one person, or 2, 3 or 4. I'll look at the most frequent number, or numbers,

5
00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:52.750
take the middle one, say, and make that the default in the stepper. So if that number is 2, and most users enter 2, 3 or 1, a user arriving will either continue straight

6
00:00:52.750 --> 00:01:04.500
away because 2 is already the default, or tap plus once or minus once, or twice, and reach the number they want. So I reduce the interaction cost we talked about at the start.

7
00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:14.500
That's another benefit of the input stepper, besides being very easy to use; anyone can use it, because it has very clear signifiers like plus and minus: signifiers that tell me what

8
00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:24.500
action will happen when I tap. I know this one increases and that one decreases, and almost everyone understands that, or the up arrow and down arrow, and so on. So it's very

9
00:01:24.500 --> 00:01:34.500
easy to use in the first place. And the interaction cost, as we said: if I set a suitable default, tapping plus or minus once gets me to the number I want, instead

10
00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:44.500
of tapping and typing, or opening a dropdown and choosing, and so on. So I have a much lower interaction cost. Like here: I need to choose how many people are travelling; I

11
00:01:44.500 --> 00:01:54.500
could open a dropdown, or open the scrolling picker and pick a number from it, or type the number myself. Using the stepper can be much faster, so it's a low interaction cost.

12
00:01:54.500 --> 00:02:05.300
If we think from the other side, what drawbacks can a stepper have? We can think about an example like this one: we find that often, especially on desktop, the stepper

13
00:02:05.300 --> 00:02:16.100
sits in a small spot, like this: an up arrow and a down arrow close together. According to Fitts's law, as we saw when we talked about it in the visual

14
00:02:16.100 --> 00:02:26.900
design course, the bigger the target I'm heading for, the faster I can get to it and click, and so the faster I finish the task or flow I'm in. So

15
00:02:26.900 --> 00:02:37.700
of course the stepper on desktop is sometimes a bit small, and getting to it and clicking can be a bit hard at times. Another point: I can't adjust numbers by

16
00:02:37.700 --> 00:02:48.500
a large amount. If I'm at 1384, say, and want to reach 1500, and I only have the stepper, I'll have to click 116 times to reach 1500, which certainly isn't practical.

17
00:02:48.500 --> 00:02:59.500
So we need to think about what we can adjust or add to the stepper to improve it or fix its problems or drawbacks. The first thing that may come to mind is a text field

18
00:02:59.500 --> 00:03:10.500
stepper: I have both options, I use it as a stepper, tapping plus or minus to raise or lower the number, or I tap it and it becomes a text field, I delete the number and type

19
00:03:10.500 --> 00:03:21.500
any number I want, for the large adjustments we mentioned. The second thing we can consider is making a long press on plus or minus raise or lower the number quickly: it might go up by

20
00:03:21.500 --> 00:03:32.500
one every second, and as I keep pressing, by one every half second, say, and so on. So it goes up and down faster while I hold plus. One more thing we can use on desktop specifically

21
00:03:32.500 --> 00:03:43.500
is the keyboard: if I press the up or down arrow while I'm on the number in the stepper, it starts to go up or down, without needing to move the mouse and click plus and minus.

22
00:03:43.500 --> 00:03:53.405
Another guideline for input steppers: we always need to make clear what value the stepper stands for. If it's a currency, I need to show exactly which one: is

23
00:03:53.405 --> 00:04:03.310
this stepper in dollars, pounds, or what? I also need to make its maximum and minimum limits clear: if I reach the maximum, the plus becomes disabled and I

24
00:04:03.310 --> 00:04:13.215
can't tap it, or if I'm already at the minimum, the minus is disabled. Like this example: adults reached the maximum, which is 14, so the plus became disabled,

25
00:04:13.215 --> 00:04:23.120
and children are at the minimum, zero, and I can't go below that, so the minus is disabled too. That's how I've made the minimum and maximum limits clear.
